<com:TContent ID="body" >

<h1>Data Controls</h1>

<p class="block-content">
Data controls are used to display a repeated content like a list or a table. The content is generated from a collection of items containing the data, called the <tt>DataSource</tt>, and a template describing the appearance, called <tt>Renderer</tt>.
<br/>
The process of assigning a DataSource to a Data control and render the template for each item is called <tt>data binding</tt>.
</p>

<ul id="u1" class="block-content">
  <li>
  <a href="?page=Controls.DataList">TDataList</a> is used to display or modify a list of data items.
  </li>

  <li>
  <a href="?page=Controls.DataGrid">TDataGrid</a> displays data in a tabular format with rows and columns.
  </li>

  <li>
  <a href="?page=Controls.Repeater">TRepeater</a> displays its content defined in templates repeatedly based on the given data.
  </li>
</ul>

<p class="block-content">
The three controls share the data binding cycle and differ in the markup they produce. A
repeater writes only what its templates say. A data list adds a layout of rows and
columns. A data grid builds a table from a set of column definitions.
</p>

<table class="parameters">
<tr><th>Control</th><th>Produces</th><th>Choose it when</th></tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>TRepeater</tt></td>
	<td>Whatever its templates contain, with no wrapper of its own</td>
	<td>The markup has to be exact, such as a list or a run of cards.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>TDataList</tt></td>
	<td>A table or a run of items, arranged by <tt>RepeatColumns</tt> and <tt>RepeatDirection</tt></td>
	<td>The items need a multi-column layout, selection, or in-place editing.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>TDataGrid</tt></td>
	<td>A table whose columns come from column controls</td>
	<td>The data is tabular and needs sorting, paging, or editing per row.</td>
</tr>
</table>

<h2>Item renderers</h2>

<p class="block-content">
Each control repeats a template for every item. A template written inline covers the
simple case. An <tt>ItemRenderer</tt> moves that template into its own file with a class
beside it, so the same item appearance is reused across pages and its logic lives in PHP
rather than in expressions. The <a href="?page=Controls.DataList">TDataList</a> and
<a href="?page=Controls.DataGrid">TDataGrid</a> pages each show one.
</p>

<h2>Paging</h2>

<p class="block-content">
<a href="?page=Controls.Pager">TPager</a> provides the page navigation for a data bound
control. A data grid also carries pagers of its own, at the top, the bottom, or both.
</p>

<h2>Supplying the data</h2>

<p class="block-content">
<tt>DataSource</tt> takes the items. It accepts an array, any object that implements
<tt>Traversable</tt>, and a <tt>TDbDataReader</tt>, which the control reads in full
because a reader is forward-only. A string is accepted too, and is split on commas into a
list. Anything else raises a <tt>TInvalidDataTypeException</tt>.
</p>

<com:TTextHighlighter Language="php" CssClass="source block-content">
$this-&gt;ProductList-&gt;DataSource = $rows;
$this-&gt;ProductList-&gt;dataBind();
</com:TTextHighlighter>

<p class="block-content">
Calling <tt>dataBind()</tt> walks the source and creates one item per entry. The control
raises <tt>OnDataBound</tt> when that finishes, and <tt>OnDataSourceChanged</tt> whenever
the source is replaced.
</p>

<p class="block-content">
<tt>DataSourceID</tt> is the alternative to setting the source in code. It holds the id
path of a control that implements <tt>IDataSource</tt>, found through the binding
control's naming container. An id that names no control, or names one that is not a data
source, raises a <tt>TInvalidDataValueException</tt>. The framework ships
<tt>TDataSourceControl</tt> as an abstract base and no concrete data source control of its
own, so this route needs a class the application supplies. <tt>DataMember</tt> names which
view of that source to read. Most applications set <tt>DataSource</tt> instead.
</p>

<h2>Paging</h2>

<p class="block-content">
All three controls inherit paging from <tt>TDataBoundControl</tt>. Paging holds back part
of the bound data instead of rendering every item.
</p>

<table class="parameters">
<tr><th>Property</th><th>Purpose</th></tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>AllowPaging</tt></td>
	<td>Turns paging on. Defaults to false.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>PageSize</tt></td>
	<td>The number of items on a page. Defaults to 10.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>CurrentPageIndex</tt></td>
	<td>The page being shown, counted from zero.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>PageCount</tt></td>
	<td>Read-only. The number of pages the current data divides into.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>AllowCustomPaging</tt></td>
	<td>Turns on custom paging, described below. Defaults to false.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>VirtualItemCount</tt></td>
	<td>The total number of items, which the application states when custom paging is on.</td>
</tr>
</table>

<h2>Ordinary paging and custom paging</h2>

<p class="block-content">
With <tt>AllowPaging</tt> alone, the control still receives every item and renders one
page of them. This is simple and costs memory in proportion to the whole data set.
</p>

<p class="block-content">
Custom paging moves the slicing to the data source. With <tt>AllowCustomPaging</tt> also
true, the control renders every item it is given and treats that as the current page. The
application fetches only that slice, and sets <tt>VirtualItemCount</tt> so the control
knows how many pages exist. For a database that means a query with a limit and an offset,
plus a second query counting the rows.
</p>

<table class="parameters">
<tr><th></th><th>Ordinary paging</th><th>Custom paging</th></tr>
<tr>
	<td>What <tt>DataSource</tt> holds</td>
	<td>Every item</td>
	<td>One page of items</td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td>Who slices the data</td>
	<td>The control</td>
	<td>The application</td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>VirtualItemCount</tt></td>
	<td>Unused</td>
	<td>Required, or the control cannot count pages</td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td>Rebinding on a page change</td>
	<td>Optional</td>
	<td>Required, to fetch the new slice</td>
</tr>
</table>

<h2>Showing the page controls</h2>

<p class="block-content">
<a href="?page=Controls.DataGrid">TDataGrid</a> renders pagers of its own, which
<tt>PagerStyle</tt> configures and <tt>TopPager</tt> and <tt>BottomPager</tt> expose.
<tt>TDataList</tt> and <tt>TRepeater</tt> render none, so a page pairs them with a
separate <a href="?page=Controls.Pager">TPager</a>. The pager's
<tt>ControlToPaginate</tt> holds the id path of the control it drives, and its
<tt>OnPageIndexChanged</tt> handler moves the control to the new page.
</p>

<com:TTextHighlighter Language="prado" CssClass="source block-content">
&lt;com:TPager ControlToPaginate="ProductList" Mode="Numeric"
    OnPageIndexChanged="pageChanged" /&gt;
&lt;com:TDataList ID="ProductList" AllowPaging="true" PageSize="5"&gt;
  ...
&lt;/com:TDataList&gt;
</com:TTextHighlighter>

<com:TTextHighlighter Language="php" CssClass="source block-content">
public function pageChanged($sender, $param)
{
    $this-&gt;ProductList-&gt;CurrentPageIndex = $param-&gt;NewPageIndex;
    $this-&gt;populateData();
}
</com:TTextHighlighter>

<p class="block-content">
Several pagers may drive one control at once. The
<a href="?page=Controls.Samples.TPager.Sample1">TPager sample</a> puts four of them around
a single data list running in custom paging mode.
</p>

</com:TContent>
